# HTTPS on a bare public IP with no DNS and no public certificate authority.
#
# The application only checks the *scheme* of APP_ORIGIN, so an HTTPS origin is accepted on a
# public address whether or not a public CA signed the certificate. `tls internal` makes Caddy
# issue one from its own local CA: browsers warn once per host:port and the participant proceeds,
# and from then on the traffic is encrypted. That matters because each participant pastes their own
# Galileo API key into the portal.
#
# What this protects against and what it does not: a self-signed certificate stops passive
# sniffing, which is the realistic risk on shared wifi. It does not prove the server's identity, so
# an attacker already positioned to intercept could still substitute their own. For a workshop on
# synthetic data that is a reasonable place to stop.
#
#   1. Stacks listen on loopback, Caddy is the only public listener:
#        python3 scripts/workshop.py up --count 50 \
#          --host <public-ip> --base-port 4100 --bind 127.0.0.1 \
#          --origin-template 'https://<public-ip>:31{n:02d}'
#
#      Participant 7 browses https://<public-ip>:3107, which Caddy proxies to 127.0.0.1:4107.
#      The template builds the *public* port from the participant number, because `{port}` is the
#      stack's internal port and the two deliberately differ.
#
#   2. Generate one block per participant, substituting the address. The ports are computed with
#      arithmetic rather than by pasting digits together: `seq 1 50` yields `1`, not `01`, and
#      "31" . "1" is port 311, which Caddy binds without complaint while nothing reaches it.
#        IP=<public-ip>
#        { printf '{\n\tauto_https disable_redirects\n}\n'
#          for i in $(seq 1 50); do
#            printf '%s:%d {\n\ttls internal\n\treverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:%d\n}\n' \
#              "$IP" $((3100+i)) $((4100+i))
#          done
#        } | sudo tee /etc/caddy/Caddyfile >/dev/null
#
#   3. sudo systemctl restart caddy
#
#      Then confirm the listeners are where you meant them to be, before trusting a browser test:
#        sudo ss -ltn | grep -E ':(31[0-9][0-9])'
#
#   4. Open 3101-3150 in the security group. Port 80 is not needed: a local CA issues the
#      certificate, so there is no HTTP challenge.
#
# Participants see a certificate warning the first time and click through. Tell them in advance,
# or the first five minutes are spent on it.

{
	# No ACME account is involved; a local CA signs everything.
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# Example for one participant. Generate the rest with the loop above.
203.0.113.10:3101 {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4101
}
